WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.819
It's great to welcome to the program today,
David Shaw, who's a data scientist who consults

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:11.190
with Democratic groups and has a number of
insights into many different areas of the

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.380
political world is the way I say it.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.670
Glad, glad to have you here today.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.580
Pleasure to be here.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.960
So let's just start with 20, 20 polling.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.350
I mean, how how off or on was it?

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:29.519
Certainly Joe Biden was winning the popular
vote polling and he won the popular vote and

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:30.779
he became the president.

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:35.910
There were certainly some states in which
the numbers leading up to the election did

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.000
not quite work out.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.430
I mean, what's your sort of overall assessment
of what's happening with polling right now?

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:47.890
Yeah, well, first, as as a, I guess, disclaimer,
I think in some ways it's funny that going

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:53.630
into election night, we thought that Joe Biden
would win and the Democrats would get 50 or

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:54.630
51 Senate seats.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.440
And, you know, the end result, Joe Biden as
president, Democrats do a 50 Senate seats.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.149
So I think that is kind of funny.

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:05.532
But, you know, that said, the polling was
actually fairly bad this year, you know, just

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:06.900
to put numbers on it.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.009
I think there's kind of two distinct problems
with polling.

00:01:11.009 --> 00:01:17.260
One is just that we really the numbers were
way too high for Democrats.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.829
On average, there was about a three percent
bias toward Democrats.

00:01:20.829 --> 00:01:24.570
And that's in vote share, which means that
in terms of margin, you're looking at roughly

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.680
six percent.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.030
This happened basically every level of government
on the Senate, on the House, you know, and

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.030
the presidency.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.210
And, you know, if you were looking at polls,
you would you are coming in expecting that

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.810
Biden was going to win states like Florida,
that we would have, you know, maybe like a

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.060
bunch of Democratic seats and that we were
supposed to pick up 10 seats in the House.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.250
And, you know, none of those things happened.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.840
And then I think on top of that, it's not
just that the polling on average was was too

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.350
democratic, but the kind of error pattern
by state, you know, really matched what we

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.350
saw in 2016.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:03.259
And, you know, what we saw in twenty eighteen
where these kind these states in the Midwest

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:09.769
with a lot of, you know, noncollege educated
whites, really, we saw a strong overestimation

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:10.769
of support.

00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:15.170
I think there was a, you know, a Quinnipiac
poll or in Wisconsin right before the election

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.540
where it said that Biden would win by 15 and
he ended up winning by less than one.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.660
So, yeah, I think it was a pretty bad cycle
for polling.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.620
And then the just the last angle to recount
all the ways the polls were bad this year

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.660
is just in terms of looking at coalitions.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.780
You know, the big changes in this election,
roughly speaking, were that non college whites

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.870
were basically where they were in 2016.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.150
College educated whites swung something like
six or seven percent toward us.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.330
You know, African-Americans swung two points
down.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.230
And I think Hispanic voters, you know, now
that all the data is in, it looks like they

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.629
dropped by like nine percent and the polls
didn't really see any of those trends coming.

00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:54.019
There was like a little bit of warning signs,
you know, with nonwhite voters that it ended

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:55.440
up being worse than the polls thought.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.140
But if you looked at the polls, the polls
really told this story that we were going

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.260
to see education depolarization, that noncollege
whites were going to trend toward Democrats

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:08.129
and college educated whites were going to
stay basically where they were last time.

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:09.310
And that just didn't happen.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.500
You know, if you look at if you just look
at the college noncollege gap, it looks like

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.520
the polls were up by something like 10 or
11 percent, which is crazy.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.120
And a lot of ways the polls were a lot worse
than 2016.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.500
So let's dig into one aspect of this, which
is that the concept of polling is you can't

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:25.530
poll everybody.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.560
So the idea is you get a representative sample.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.599
And what does it mean that it's representative?

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:34.610
Often we're talking about, well, we'll normalize
it for education or we can normalize it for

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.610
race.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.150
We could normalize it for political affiliation.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.120
You can normalize it for age, all of these
different things.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.819
But one of the things you really can't normalize
for, correct me if I'm wrong, is the types

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:49.030
of people that are more likely to participate
in a poll versus the types of people that

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.810
are more likely to opt out.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:57.510
And we heard this from some on the right who
who kind of said it as a lot of Trump ists

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.640
don't trust the polls.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.850
So they either give deliberately fake answers
or don't participate.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:06.909
And there's no great way to deal with that.

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:12.700
As a pollster, how much truth is there to
the general idea of people willing to and

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.730
not willing to participate and then maybe
more specifically to some of the explanations

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:20.760
that were given by supporters of the former
President Trump?

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:26.610
Yeah, I think in some ways I think there's
something to the story, I do want to be clear

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.830
that if you ask someone who they're going
to vote for in a survey, they do generally

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:32.270
tell you the truth.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.370
It's not an issue trolling.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:38.900
But, you know, that said, I think one of the
big drivers for why the polling was so wrong

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:44.760
this year and why it was actually worse than
2016 was that there were really giant differences

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.460
in who was answering the phone.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.950
You know, something that you know, the way
that you know, the firm I worked for, the

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.190
way that they do polling, they actually match
people back to voter files.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.910
And so we can see their party registration.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.630
And something that was really clear is that
starting around March, Democrats became much

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.330
more likely, you know, to answer the phone
than Republicans.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.700
And I think it's they kind of stayed at these
elevated levels throughout the entire election.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:12.460
And I think there's a pretty clear story that
this had a lot to do with lock down, that

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.460
Democrats were literally home.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.760
There's like a lot of cell phone mobility
data that really shows that Democrats stayed

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.680
home and they didn't really have much to do.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.290
And they started answering surveys at high
rates and Republicans weren't doing that.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:26.260
And, you know, they just weren't picking up
the phones.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.380
But, you know, generalizing, you know, that
was an issue with this year.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.420
And I think it was clear.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:35.540
But even in 2016, you know, when we look back
at why the polls were wrong and I think this

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:41.430
bias continue now, like why is it that pollsters
have such a hard time measuring opinion in

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.320
states that have a lot of working class white
people?

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.500
And originally, people really talked about
this in terms of Trump turning out a lot of

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:50.500
new voters.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:53.700
And I think that there's a lot of evidence
now that that's not really true, that, you

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.690
know, all of these elections, which is really
driven by people changing their minds as opposed

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:58.840
to changes in who voted.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:04.700
But then the question is, why did why were
the polls wrong and why were the polls wrong

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.520
in more in working class areas?

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.340
And it kind of comes back to this concept
of social trust that political scientists

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.480
and sociologists like to talk about.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:18.400
And so that's just operationalized with this
question of do you think that people can be

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.200
trusted or do you think that people should
keep to themselves?

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.670
And something that's pretty wild, just socially
is that, you know, the government's been asking

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.770
this on a very high quality survey.

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:31.130
They spend a couple of thousand dollars per
person and they get like a 70 percent response

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:32.130
rate.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.150
Phone response rates are closer to one percent
just for reference point.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.080
And they've been asking it since the 70s.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.180
And it used to be that, you know, roughly
55 percent of people said that people could

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.200
be trusted and that figure is down to 30 percent.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.840
So that's sociologically that's sociologically
very concerning, though, above my pay grade.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:56.520
But it has measurement implications because
people who trust people who say that people

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.260
can be trusted, kind of unsurprisingly, are
a lot more likely to answer phone surveys.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:06.080
And that used to not matter very much because
how much you trusted the people around you

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.530
and how much you trust institutions just used
to not be that correlated with partizanship.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:15.510
But in 2016, you know, Donald Trump really
reshaped the coalition.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.530
You know, 2012 was a poll.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:22.660
It was basically, you know, a fight over whether
or not we should have universal health care.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:27.310
2016 was much more of a referendum on like
how much do you trust the people around you?

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:31.930
And we were only talking in these surveys
to these high trust people who say that people

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:33.350
can be trusted.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:37.920
And if you and, you know, there was literally
this hidden, you know, hidden majority of

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:40.630
low trust folks who went in the other direction.

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.480
And so, you know, we've actually looked at
crosstab.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.100
And if you look among people who said that
people could be trusted, that group swung

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.490
toward Democrats by, you know, something like
four or five percent.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.150
But the other group went completely in the
opposite direction.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.200
And, you know, this high trust sample, that
was the people we were polling and that's

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:59.130
the world we expected to see on Election Day.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.480
But then they didn't actually answer.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.180
And, you know, the same thing.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:06.030
You know, I actually you know, people don't
realize what happened in twenty eighteen Democrats

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.200
won, so they didn't notice.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.730
But if you look at the actual margins in places
like West Virginia, places like Michigan,

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:15.000
places like Ohio, the elections were much
closer than people.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.670
You know, the polls originally thought, even
if even if we ended up winning and then we

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:24.930
see the same thing again, you know, in in
2020.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:29.760
And and I think that social justice is a very
important angle for understanding it.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:34.630
One of the things I saw you to shift gears
a little bit, I read some of your tweets about,

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:39.140
which I find very interesting, is about the
bias of the Electoral College.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:41.580
And you posted a couple of things that I find
really interesting.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:45.940
One is that you posted the partizan bias of
the Electoral College over time.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:51.320
And one of the things you found is that Democrats
now need about 52 percent of the vote in order

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:56.890
to have a 50/50 shot of winning the presidency,
given that it's not the national popular vote

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.430
that decides, but rather the electoral vote.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:04.100
And then the other really interesting thing
is that there's this notion that part of what

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:10.270
makes the Electoral College depart in terms
of its orientation from the popular vote is

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:13.880
that small states are really overweighted
relative to their populations.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:18.850
And you pointed out something very interesting,
which is that in terms of the difference makers,

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.570
when it comes down to who ultimately.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:22.590
He wins the Electoral College.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:28.620
It's more about the fact that there are some
larger population states which are relatively

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:32.860
close, could be 52, 48, could be 55, 45, 60,
40.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:37.510
But because they are winner take all, you
are essentially I don't know if the right

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:42.990
word is this disenfranchizing, but if you're
part of the 42 percent of Democrats in a big

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:48.020
red state, your vote essentially doesn't matter
for calculating the Electoral College.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:51.090
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:58.390
Yeah, absolutely, I think it's just important
to say that the bias in the Electoral College,

00:09:58.390 --> 00:09:59.390
I think is very new.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:03.521
You know, it is true that, you know, Gore
won the popular vote and lost the Electoral

00:10:03.521 --> 00:10:07.450
College, but the bias of the Electoral College
was really quite small that year.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:10.460
You know, Gore got something like fifty two
point two percent.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:13.150
And, you know, he had a very, very narrowly
lost.

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:17.180
And so that, you know, that was like random
and not really that big a deal.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.870
And really, the Electoral College at various
points is favor Democrats.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:26.590
You know, it's if if Barack Obama in 2012
had gotten about forty nine point five percent

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:29.200
of the vote, he actually still probably would
have won.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.131
That's that's why people talk about the blue
wall there.

00:10:31.131 --> 00:10:34.860
There are basically these big this is just
a little bit of a history lesson.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:39.700
But, you know, there there are just these
big Midwestern states that kind of had their

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:42.300
economy centered around farming and manufacturing.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:47.030
And as a result, you know, they have their
both, you know, relatively white and also

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:49.960
have relatively low education levels relative
to the rest of the country.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:55.720
And the Electoral College really comes down
to who does well in these places.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:59.340
And in 2012, you know, Obama, a lot of his
appeal was he actually did very well, you

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:00.420
know, with Midwestern whites.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:05.330
He did better in the Midwest than Kerry and
really than a lot of Democrats before him.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:08.940
And but he also did well enough among college
educated whites to make places like Virginia

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:09.960
and Colorado competitive.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.370
So the Electoral College had like a small
bias in his favor.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:15.610
2016 totally changed that, though.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.460
And, you know, it's really great if you can
see the graph because, you know, it's kind

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:23.340
of wiggles around zero and then there's just
the sudden plunge to their being, I think

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:28.290
a three point, you know, three point gap bias
in the Republican favor.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:33.760
And I think that that's, you know, the really
underappreciated part of understanding 2016,

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:40.750
which is that Barack Obama got about 52 percent
of the two party vote in 2012 and Hillary

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.700
Clinton got fifty one point one percent of
the vote.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.870
And in any other democracy, that would have
been fine, you know, zero point nine percent

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:51.440
decline still, you know, comfortably above
50.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:56.040
But the problem was that the Electoral College
shifted from being about half a point, biased

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:00.210
in favor of Democrats to being three points
biased in favor of Republicans.

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:04.250
And then that bias increased again.

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:06.460
And so it's helpful to put numbers on these
things.

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:11.220
And, you know, just to get to your other question,
I think people really don't understand why

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:12.850
the Electoral College causes this bias.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:16.910
You know, as you said, it's not that you have
these tiny states.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:18.410
It's not about Wyoming.

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:24.230
In order to get to 270 states, you have to
win in states like Wisconsin, like Minnesota,

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:25.279
like Ohio.

00:12:25.279 --> 00:12:29.690
And there just happened to be a bunch of relatively
evenly divided states that right now are somewhat

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:32.070
more Republican than the country overall.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:36.890
And I think that, you know, the key point
is that the reason why this happened is because

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:41.550
Democrats have started doing so much worse
among working class white people.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:45.750
And, you know, unless that gets reversed,
I think it's unlikely that this is going to

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:46.750
that this is going to reverse.

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:50.180
You know, I think a lot of people expected
that, you know, things would get better this

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:51.180
year.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:56.660
And the key point is that the bias went from
three points in 2016 to four points in 2020.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:02.720
And so it really just shows how unworkable
our coalition is under this set of rules.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:07.910
Absolutely fundamental insights to understand
really and thinking about future elections.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:12.700
I mean, if you if one does not understand
reality as it is, it's hard to run a campaign

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:15.130
that that is based on that reality.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:19.541
We've been speaking with David Shorr, who's
a data scientist who consults with democratic

00:13:19.541 --> 00:13:20.541
groups.

00:13:20.541 --> 00:13:23.210
You certainly should follow him on Twitter,
as I do.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.360
David, I so appreciate your time today.

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:26.600
Thank you so much.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:27.839
Pleasure to be here.

